Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The last strategy succeeded syntactically but underperformed semantically:
// many outputs collapsed to short prefixes such as `SELECT <id>`. That pattern
// indicates we were closing too eagerly once the grammar admitted a complete
// query prefix, and we also delegated too much to generic constrained decoding
// without explicitly biasing toward richer SQL continuations. On Spider, many
// questions require clauses beyond the earliest complete query, e.g. FROM,
// WHERE, GROUP BY, ORDER BY, joins, or aggregates tied to schema tokens.
//
// This revision keeps the "open immediately" behavior, but changes the
// constrained policy in two important ways.
//
// First, when inside the SQL span, we directly manipulate logits to strongly
// prefer parser-valid continuations and to specifically boost structurally
// informative SQL tokens such as FROM, WHERE, GROUP, ORDER, BY, JOIN, ON,
// HAVING, LIMIT, DESC, ASC, and aggregate operators. We also heavily penalize
// eosToken while the parser is not complete so generation does not terminate
// before a full query is formed. The next token is then sampled from these
// adjusted logits and appended only if parser-valid.
//
// Second, even when the parser reports that the current prefix is complete, we
// do not immediately close. Instead we spend one decision step asking whether a
// high-value valid continuation still exists. If such a continuation is valid,
// we append it and keep expanding the query; otherwise we close the span. This
// directly targets the observed `SELECT <id>` failure mode by preferring
// continuation into the relational skeleton of a full SQL statement rather than
// stopping at the first grammatically complete fragment.
//
// The overall effect is an "open immediately, then validity-filtered
// SQL-structure-biased expansion, and close only when no strong continuation is
// available" strategy, which better matches single-query Spider decoding.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: The open branch uses OpenConstrainedSpan, which enters with
//   currentConstrainedOut := [] and [] is a valid parser prefix. In the close
//   branch, CloseConstrainedSpan makes insideConstrainedOut false, so the
//   implication is vacuous. In the constrained append branches, we first check
//   IsTokenValidNext(parser, currentConstrainedOut, next); only then do we call
//   AppendConstrainedToken, which preserves parser-validity of the updated
//   currentConstrainedOut. Break branches leave the state unchanged.
//
// suffix: After opening, currentConstrainedOut is [], so the suffix property is
//   immediate. After closing, insideConstrainedOut is false, so the implication
//   is vacuous. In every append branch, AppendConstrainedToken appends the same
//   valid token to generated and to the constrained suffix, so
//   generated[|generated|-|currentConstrainedOut|..] == currentConstrainedOut
//   remains true. Break branches do not modify generated or currentConstrainedOut.
//
// cost accounting: `cost` stays 0 throughout the loop and is assigned once at
//   the end to `steps`. OpenConstrainedSpan and CloseConstrainedSpan consume one
//   step and we increment steps by 1. In each manual logit-decoding branch we
//   call lm.ChooseNextToken() exactly once, manually bump helpers.cost by 1, and
//   increment steps by 1; if the sampled token is rejected we break without
//   changing the generated text, but the step has still been accounted for.
//   Thus final cost := steps satisfies cost <= maxSteps.
//
// progress: OpenConstrainedSpan appends exactly one token and we increment steps
//   by 1. CloseConstrainedSpan also appends exactly one token and increments
//   steps by 1. In each constrained sampling branch, either we append exactly
//   one token via AppendConstrainedToken and increment steps by 1, or we break
//   immediately after the one decoding step with generated unchanged. Therefore
//   |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps is preserved by simple linear
//   arithmetic in every branch.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
