// The previous strategy correctly enforced SQL syntax but still produced the same
// minimal pattern (`SELECT <id> FROM <id>`) on most examples. The key bug is not
// merely "closing too early": the old loop explicitly broke whenever the current
// constrained prefix was complete but not forced to close. That means the method
// often stopped inside the constrained span with a short complete SQL query and
// never spent remaining budget on richer clauses. Evaluation therefore saw many
// valid-but-underfit SQL programs.
//
// This revision changes the constrained policy in two important ways.
//
// 1. Complete prefixes are no longer terminal by default.
//    When the SQL prefix is complete, we now continue generation if there is
//    budget and the parser still offers valid continuations. We only close the
//    constrained span when the budget is nearly exhausted or when there are no
//    valid continuations to pursue. This directly targets the observed
//    under-generation failure mode.
//
// 2. Constrained generation uses parser-safe token acquisition every iteration.
//    Rather than sampling arbitrary logits and then often collapsing to a break,
//    the loop asks for parser-valid candidates, boosts them strongly, samples,
//    validates, and otherwise falls back to `ConstrainedStep`. This keeps syntax
//    perfect while making it much more likely that generation actually advances.
//    If the fallback returns EOS, we close only when the current prefix is
//    already complete; otherwise we stop safely.
//
// Unconstrained behavior is still simple: decode until `<<`, then perform all SQL
// generation inside the constrained span. This preserves the required delimiters
// while focusing the strategy on the benchmark's real issue: spending available
// budget to elaborate beyond the shortest complete SQL skeleton.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the unconstrained chunk branch we stay outside constrained
//   mode, so the implication is unchanged; opening a span yields the empty
//   constrained prefix, which is valid. In the constrained branch, query helpers
//   do not change state; AppendConstrainedToken is used only with a token proved
//   valid or returned by ConstrainedStep, so the new constrained prefix is valid.
//   CloseConstrainedSpan sets insideConstrainedOut to false, making the invariant
